     "page": 1,
     "text": "FLYNN-PATEL CLINIC - DISCHARGE SUMMARY\n\nPatient: Amy Erickson DOB: 08/04/1994 Sex: F MRN: 389386\nAdmit: 12/30/2013 Discharge: 01/03/2014\n\nPrincipal diagnosis: Pancreatitis (chronic; cause other)\nSecondary diagnosis: None\n\nHospital course: Ms. Erickson, a 29-year-old female, was admitted on December 2013, with a diagnosis of chronic pancreatitis, the cause of which remains unspecified. The patient has a known history of chronic pancreatitis, first on December 30, 2013. Upon her vital signs were stable with a blood pressure of 122/80 mmHg and a weight of pounds. She reported no acute pain but noted a mild pruritic rash, which was evaluated and deemed \n\nThroughout her hospital stay, Ms. Erickson remained hemodynamically stable. Her rash was managed with topical agents, and she did not require any specific treatment for her chronic pancreatitis during this admission. No acute interventions were necessary. \n\nA comprehensive review of systems was performed, revealing no significant changes or new symptoms. The patient's family history is notable for hypertension in her mother, who was diagnosed at age 75. Ms. Erickson denies any personal history of hypertension or other significant medical conditions. \n\nSocial history indicates that Ms. Erickson is a current smoker, consuming approximately 5 cigarettes per day since July 24, 2009. She also reports moderate alcohol consumption, approximately 2 drinks per week. \n\nDuring her stay, various routine laboratory tests were all of which returned within normal limits. An EKG was performed to rule out any cardiac abnormalities, and it was found to be normal (r/o Abnormal EKG). \n\nMs. Erickson was discharged home on January 3, 2014, in stable condition. She was advised to continue her current management plan for chronic pancreatitis and to follow up with her gastroenterologist, Dr. Jessica Harrison, at the Flynn-Patel Clinic. \n\nDischarge medications: None specific for pancreatitis. Routine medications and over-the-counter agents for symptom management as needed.\n\nFollow-up: Ms. Erickson is scheduled to follow up with Dr. Harrison in two weeks to reassess her condition and manage her chronic pancreatitis. She was also advised to seek immediate medical attention if experiences any acute abdominal pain, fever, or other concerning symptoms.\n\nIn summary, Ms. Erickson's hospital course was uneventful, and she was discharged in stable condition with a plan for close outpatient follow-up.\n"
